report a send to a class that is not initialised yet

The message send trace was structurally blind to exactly the sends it is most often used to look
for. lookUpImpOrForward sets LOOKUP_NOCACHE when the receiver class is not initialised yet:

    if (slowpath(!cls->isInitialized())) {
        // The first message sent to a class is often +new or +alloc, or +self
        ...
        behavior |= LOOKUP_NOCACHE;
    }

and the done block only calls log_and_fill_cache when that flag is CLEAR. So the first message sent
to a class, which is the one that answers "was this class ever reached", was never reported.

Measured on iA Writer with CIDER_TRACE_MSGSEND=IAText: 533 sends recorded before, 591 after, and
IATextNormalization went from ABSENT to eleven lines, the first of them +initialize, which is the
pre initialisation window itself.

This matters beyond one application. A trace that answers a question with silence has to be able to
speak first, and this one could not for a whole class of sends.

Logging without caching is the whole change: the NOCACHE decision is deliberate, so it is left
alone.

--- a/runtime/objc-runtime-new.mm
+++ b/runtime/objc-runtime-new.mm
@@ -6505,6 +6505,14 @@
         }
 #endif
         log_and_fill_cache(cls, imp, sel, inst, curClass);
+    } else if (slowpath(objcMsgLogWanted() && curClass)) {
+        /*
+         * A send to a class that is not initialized yet takes LOOKUP_NOCACHE above, and that is
+         * exactly the FIRST send to that class, which is what a trace asking "was this ever called"
+         * needs most. Report it without caching it.
+         */
+        logMessageSend(curClass->isMetaClass(), cls->nameForLogging(),
+                       curClass->nameForLogging(), sel);
     }
  done_unlock:
     runtimeLock.unlock();
